# f1 = spread, f2 = count
# a pair `[whole, whole / 2]` lies in storage of doubles. By its written place the first element is a whole number. Where its only user reckons with a fraction the read takes the form of a double, as the second does, and claims
# no more than a number: the emitter holds a double read off a list to what is claimed of it. NOT SO where both operands are whole numbers: there the read stays as it was.
